Project Overview

The project adds three major security layers to xv6:

1. User Authentication and RBAC
2. File Access Control
3. System Call Audit Logging

Together, these layers simulate how a real medical device operating system could protect sensitive patient information and device settings.

The system uses three different user roles:

* ADMIN
* DOCTOR
* PATIENT

Each role has different permissions and access levels.

Role System

ADMIN (UID 0)

* Full access to the entire system
* Bypasses all permission checks
* Can read audit logs
* Can manage users

DOCTOR (UID 2)

* Can read patient records
* Can read and write dosage logs
* Cannot access device configuration

PATIENT (UID 1)

* Can read personal records
* Can read insulin dosage information
* Cannot modify device settings
* Cannot access audit logs

Development Environment

The project was built and tested using:

* Kali Linux
* QEMU
* RISC-V 64-bit architecture
* ANSI C
* xv6-riscv

How the System Works

The project starts by forcing every user to authenticate before accessing the shell.

Instead of spawning the shell immediately after boot, the modified init process displays a login prompt. The shell only starts after successful authentication through a kernel syscall.

This creates a strict security boundary between user space and kernel space.

Phase 1 — Authentication and RBAC

The first phase implements user authentication and role-based access control.

A user enters a username and password from user space, but the actual verification happens entirely inside the kernel.

The login flow works like this:

1. User enters credentials
2. User program calls login()
3. A syscall transfers execution into kernel mode
4. sys_login() validates the request
5. auth_verify() checks credentials
6. Process credentials are stored inside struct proc
7. The shell launches only after successful authentication

The kernel stores:

* UID
* GID
* User role
* Authentication state

inside the process structure itself.

This means user applications cannot forge identities or modify their own permissions.

Security Improvements Added to struct proc

The project extends struct proc by adding:

* Process credentials
* Authentication state
* Stack canary protection

The stack canary helps detect memory corruption or buffer overflow attacks inside newly added syscall handlers.

Password Storage

Passwords are hashed using the djb2 hashing algorithm.

The project intentionally documents this as an educational limitation because xv6 does not include modern cryptographic libraries.

In a production environment, this should be replaced with:

* bcrypt
* Argon2
* Per-user salts

New Authentication Syscalls

The project adds several new syscalls to xv6:

* login
* whoami
* useradd
* userdel
* passwd
* chmod
* chown
* audit_read

These syscalls required changes in:

* syscall.h
* syscall.c
* usys.pl
* user.h

The syscall stubs generated by usys.pl allow normal C functions in user space to trigger privileged kernel execution through the ecall instruction.

Phase 2 — File Access Control

The second phase implements UNIX-style permissions directly inside xv6.

The project adds:

* mode
* uid
* gid

to both:

* struct dinode (disk)
* struct inode (memory)

This allows file permissions to persist across reboots while still being cached efficiently in RAM.

Protected Medical Files

The system secures several important files:

/patient/records

* Patient records
* Readable by patient and doctor

/dosage/insulin.log

* Insulin dosage information
* Writable by doctor
* Readable by patient

/device/config

* Device configuration
* Admin only

/audit/syscall.log

* Security logs
* Admin read-only

Permission Enforcement

The project centralizes all permission logic into a single function called check_permission().

This function:

* Checks user identity
* Compares ownership
* Verifies group permissions
* Applies UNIX permission bits
* Returns allow or deny

The permission system follows this order:

1. Admin bypass
2. Owner permissions
3. Group permissions
4. Other permissions

Centralizing the logic reduces security bugs and makes maintenance easier.

Kernel-Level Protection

Permission checks happen before any actual file operation occurs.

For example:

* fileread() checks permissions before readi()
* sys_open() validates permissions before allocating a file descriptor

This guarantees unauthorized users never receive even partial file data.

Real Access Results

Doctor:

* Can read patient records
* Can read insulin logs
* Cannot access device configuration

Patient:

* Can read personal records
* Can read insulin logs
* Cannot access configuration

Admin:

* Can access everything

Phase 3 — Audit Logging

The third phase adds accountability to the system.

Every important syscall attempt is recorded inside a kernel ring buffer.

This includes:

* Successful operations
* Failed operations
* Permission denials
* Authentication attempts

The audit system records:

* PID
* UID
* Syscall name
* Timestamp
* Custom message

Only the admin user can read audit logs.

Why Logging Happens in trap.c

The audit hook is placed inside usertrap() before syscall() executes.

This is important because it guarantees every attempt is logged even if:

* The syscall fails
* The syscall exits early
* The syscall crashes

Nothing security-related happens without leaving an audit trail.

Ring Buffer Design

The audit log uses a circular buffer with 256 entries.

Advantages:

* Never blocks execution
* Old entries are overwritten automatically
* Fixed memory usage
* Safe for kernel use

Spinlocks protect the buffer from race conditions on multi-core systems.

Automated Security Testing

The project includes an automated testing program called sectest.

The test suite verifies:

* Authentication
* RBAC restrictions
* File permissions
* Audit log protection
* User privilege enforcement

A total of 12 test cases were implemented.

Test Results

The project successfully passed 9 out of 12 automated tests.

Passing tests included:

* Admin login
* Failed password rejection
* Patient login
* File permission enforcement
* Audit log restrictions
* RBAC restrictions

The remaining 3 failures were caused by a file initialization timing issue, not by incorrect permission logic.

The issue happens because:

* fsinit_security() applies permissions
* before mkfs creates the files

The actual permission enforcement itself works correctly, as confirmed by live runtime testing.

Main Security Concepts Used

Principle of Least Privilege

* Every role only receives the minimum permissions required

Defense in Depth

* Multiple independent protection layers exist simultaneously

Ring 0 Enforcement

* Security decisions happen entirely in kernel mode

Accountability

* Every important action is logged

Single Point of Truth

* All permission logic exists in one function

AAA Security Model

* Authentication
* Authorization
* Accountability

Known Limitations

Some limitations intentionally remain because xv6 is a teaching operating system.

Current limitations:

* Passwords are visible while typing
* djb2 is not cryptographically secure
* No login rate limiting
* Fixed stack canary value
* No ASLR
* Audit logs are not persisted to disk

Recommended production improvements:

* Use bcrypt or Argon2
* Randomize stack canaries
* Add login throttling
* Implement ASLR
* Save audit logs persistently

Possible Improvements

The project can likely reach full marks with two small fixes:

1. Fix file initialization order

* Ensure files exist before applying security metadata

2. Randomize stack canaries

* Generate unique canaries per process or session

Conclusion

This project demonstrates how xv6 can be extended into a security-focused operating system capable of protecting sensitive medical device data.

The implementation combines:

* Authentication
* Role-based access control
* File permissions
* Audit logging

inside the kernel itself.

The final result is a lightweight but realistic example of how operating system security concepts can be applied to embedded medical systems using xv6 and RISC-V.
